  "verbalization": "The visible evidence strongly suggests a direct relationship between K-252a and E3 ubiquitin-protein ligase UBR4, mediated through Theasaponin E1. This is supported by multiple paths showing that K-252a targets different members of the solute carrier organic anion transporter family, all of which are targeted by Theasaponin E1. Theasaponin E1, in turn, directly interacts with UBR4, indicating a common pathway where the compound acts as a bridge between K-252a and the E3 ubiquitin ligase. The consistent scores across the three paths suggest a robust, well-defined interaction, with Theasaponin E1 serving as a critical intermediary. The mechanism appears to involve the compound's ability to target multiple transporters, which may collectively influence the ubiquitination and degradation of UBR4, thereby modulating its function. This integrated pathway highlights the compound's potential to influence cellular processes through multiple molecular targets, emphasizing its role as a versatile modulator of ubiquitin-dependent pathways.",
